<section class="section">
  <div class="section-head reveal">
    <div class="title-block">
      <div class="label">questions</div>
      <h2>The common ones.</h2>
      <p>Six more on <a href="/services/#faq">/services</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="faq-strip reveal">
    <div class="faq-item">
      <div>
        <div class="q">How does pricing work?</div>
        <div class="a">Every paid engagement is quoted on scope. The free 30-minute scoping call ends with a written quote within 24 hours covering deliverables, milestones, timeline, and the total price. Once the quote is accepted and the agreement is signed, the price is locked for the agreed scope. There is no hourly billing and no scope-creep invoicing. Audit and Sprint engagements are billed 50 percent on signing and 50 percent at delivery. Custom builds are milestone-paid against a staged schedule. The Ops Retainer is billed monthly and is cancellable with 30 days' notice.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="num">01 / 10</div>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <div>
        <div class="q">Do you build on n8n, Make, or Zapier?</div>
        <div class="a">Not as the primary execution layer for production work. Visual no-code platforms add a recurring subscription that exists outside the project repository, cannot be reviewed in pull requests, and stops functioning when the subscription lapses. Vercel cron jobs and serverless functions implement the same workflow as code in the client's own repository, deployed to infrastructure the client already pays for, versioned in git, and debuggable through standard production logs. For a non-technical team operating a single workflow, a visual platform is often the right choice. For three or more workflows, or any situation where ongoing ownership matters, the code-first version produces a better result. <a href="/why-code-first/" style="color: var(--green); border-bottom: 1px dashed var(--green);">Full architectural argument here.</a></div>
      </div>
      <div class="num">02 / 10</div>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <div>
        <div class="q">What does "solo by choice" mean for delivery risk?</div>
        <div class="a">Every line of code on the engagement is written and reviewed by me directly. No subcontracting, no junior staff fill-in, no agency layer. The trade-off is that if I am unavailable for a week, the project pauses rather than being handed to a stranger who would need to ramp on the context. The engagement contract includes a refund clause for milestones that miss agreed delivery dates, and Custom builds use staged milestone payments to limit client exposure. In exchange, the engagement runs without handoff friction, without project-management overhead, and with decisions made in hours rather than weeks because there is no internal chain of approval.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="num">03 / 10</div>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <div>
        <div class="q">Can you work in our existing stack instead of yours?</div>
        <div class="a">For most modern stacks, yes: Next.js, Astro, Supabase, Django, Rails, Laravel, FastAPI, Postgres, Vercel, and Cloudflare are all in scope. If the existing stack is operating well, I will build inside it without re-platforming. The exception is when the existing stack is the root cause of the problem the engagement is meant to solve — for example, WordPress used for programmatic SEO at scale, or a critical lead pipeline running through a single Zapier zap. In those cases I will say so on the scoping call and recommend either a migration plan or a different consultant. Approximately one in five scoping calls reaches that conclusion.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="num">04 / 10</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

